  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA (descriptive, verb-disciplined) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>At the sidelines of the UN General Assembly in New York on September 22–23, Qatari, Pakistani, and Egyptian intermediaries carried a written ceasefire proposal between Iranian and US delegations in a three-hour proximity session — the first formally brokered multilateral contact since the Islamabad MoU's August expiry. Iranian FM Araghchi authored and transmitted a 60-day ceasefire roadmap to US envoys Witkoff and Kushner through the mediator corridor: the document called for a region-wide ceasefire, Hormuz reopening on day six of US acceptance, and nuclear talks beginning on day seven. On September 25–26, the Wall Street Journal and Bloomberg confirmed that Trump dismissed the proposal and briefed aides on his expectation to resume bombing Iran after the November 5 midterm elections, with scope and timing left undefined. → moved <span class="cite">F8</span></p>

      <p>On September 19, CENTCOM Commander Adm. Brad Cooper announced that US forces had supported cumulative transit of 1 billion barrels of oil through the Strait of Hormuz over two months, citing more than 2,000 commercial transits under coalition escort and claiming the prior two-week volume as the highest in six months. AIS-visible data for September 23 registered 11 transits — 9 inbound, 2 outbound — against a pre-war daily baseline of 85–125 vessels; the 10-day rolling average stood near 17–18 vessels, roughly 57% of the threshold of 30 with seven days remaining before the October 3 deadline. IRGC spokesman Mohebbi confirmed on September 26 that Revolutionary Guard operations will continue until Iran's seven conditions are met, removing any near-term Iranian commitment to halt mine operations. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>Three polls published September 22–24 showed fractured seat projections for the October 27 Israeli election contest. Channel 12 (Sep 23) placed Yashar at 23 seats and Likud at 20; Channel 13 (Sep 24) gave Likud only 18 seats and Eisenkot's bloc a 52–51 overall edge; an i24NEWS divergent poll gave Likud 27 seats and Yashar 22. Four of five surveys published across September placed Yashar first or tied for the plurality; neither main bloc reached the 61 seats required for unambiguous coalition formation in any poll. Netanyahu traveled to UNGA during this polling window and his delegation worked to block US acceptance of Iran's ceasefire roadmap, maintaining the wartime-leadership framing that anchors his campaign. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>

      <p>Netanyahu's caretaker government, holding executive authority since the Knesset's natural-term dissolution on July 17, deployed to New York for UNGA with anti-ceasefire lobbying as a central objective; reporting on September 25 described Israeli officials working to prevent the US from engaging with Araghchi's written roadmap. The caretaker posture — managing conflict parameters while campaigning — leaves Netanyahu with no new legislative mandate but full executive authority through election day. The coalition that dissolved in July never lost its Knesset majority through political fracture; it exited at the scheduled end of term. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>No US or coalition kinetic strike on Iranian territory onshore was confirmed in the 17 consecutive operational periods before September 24, the longest such pause in announced onshore strikes since the September 1 re-escalation, according to GlobalSecurity's daily operational reporting. The lull coincided with UNGA week and the proximity talks, though neither side announced a formal tactical pause. Iranian media and IRGC statements throughout the period maintained declaratory continuity on military readiness without citing new offensive kinetic operations against US assets. → supports <span class="cite">F10</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

