      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (20%):</strong> Driven primarily by F10 (p=0.12 for US onshore strikes by Oct 10) and the prospect of an Iranian provocation triggering an immediate US response outside the midterm constraint window; Trump's "could change" qualifier preserves this path.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (52%):</strong> Most probable outcome — Trump's pre-midterm restraint holds, Iran's recommitment test (F8) resolves ambiguously, Hormuz traffic stagnates near 17–20/day, and neither side achieves the preconditions its stated position requires for a ceasefire.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (28%):</strong> Requires F8 YES (Iran publicly recommits to talks) followed by a US counter-proposal rather than non-response, creating a new diplomatic track that could bring Hormuz traffic toward the F11 threshold (20/day) by mid-October.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN (descriptive, ~200 words) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>IRGC spokesman Hossein Mohebbi's September 26 statement — that the Revolutionary Guards will maintain military operations against the United States until Tehran's seven conditions are met — defined the IRGC's public framing in the hours after Trump's reported rejection of the ceasefire roadmap. The seven conditions circulated through IRNA in the days after Araghchi's UNGA appearance: complete ceasefire including Lebanon, release of $12 billion in frozen Iranian assets, lift of the US naval blockade, oil sanction waivers, security guarantees, nuclear talks resumption, and reparations. State media outlets IRNA and Tasnim ran both the Araghchi diplomatic track and the Mohebbi military track in parallel throughout the week, treating the written roadmap as a diplomatic achievement regardless of the US response, and framing Trump's rejection as confirmation of American bad faith and, in Araghchi's own UNGA press language, "economic terrorism." → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Mojtaba Khamenei's office — Supreme Leader since March 9 — has not issued a direct public statement on the ceasefire roadmap's fate as of September 26; IRNA readouts cite Supreme Leader communiqués endorsing "resistance diplomacy" and a combined negotiation-plus-deterrence posture. State media have not carried CENTCOM's 1-billion-barrel Hormuz cumulative milestone, which would acknowledge the effectiveness of US escort operations; coverage instead emphasizes the ongoing US naval blockade on Iranian oil exports, which the war-economy framing treats as evidence of US aggression. This gap between what Iranian state media broadcasts and the independent traffic data available externally functions as a domestic information-management instrument, suppressing public assessment of military attrition across the seven months of the conflict. The dual-track posture — Araghchi's diplomacy as strength, IRGC operations as deterrence — has been consistent since the Islamabad MoU's collapse. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

